#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

// Global variables for cooldown timer and attack selection
int cooldownTimer = 0;
bool attack1Selected = false;
bool attack2Selected = false;
std::mutex mtx;
std::condition_variable cv;

void selectAndExecuteAttack() {
    // Acquire lock on mutex to ensure thread-safe access to cooldown timer and attack selection variables
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lck(mtx);

    // Check if cooldown period has expired
    if (cooldownTimer < 9) {
        // Cooldown period has not expired, wait for it to expire
        cv.wait(lck, [] { return cooldownTimer >= 9; });
    }

    // Randomly select one of two attacks
    int attackSelection = rand() % 2;

    // Update attack selection and reset cooldown timer
    if (attackSelection == 0) {
        attack1Selected = true;
        cooldownTimer = 9;
    } else {
        attack2Selected = true;
        cooldownTimer = 9;
    }

    // Notify all waiting threads that the cooldown period has expired
    cv.notify_all();
}
```
This code uses a global cooldown timer to ensure that each attack can only be used once every 9 seconds. The `selectAndExecuteAttack()` function randomly selects one of two attacks, and updates the attack selection and cooldown timer accordingly. The function also uses a condition variable to wait for the cooldown period to expire before allowing another attack to be selected.

To handle potential race conditions if multiple instances of this function are called simultaneously within the 9-second window, the code uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the cooldown timer and attack selection variables at a time. The `std::condition_variable` is also used to notify all waiting threads when the cooldown period has expired.

The function gracefully handles the scenario where an attack is requested before the cooldown period has expired by waiting for the cooldown period to expire before allowing another attack to be selected. This ensures that the game character does not use multiple attacks in rapid succession, which could cause issues with the game's mechanics or balance."]

