The `DeserializationQueue` class provides a thread-safe queue for storing byte arrays that need to be deserialized. The `enqueue` method adds a new byte array to the queue, while the `dequeue` method removes and deserializes the next available byte array in the queue. If no more byte arrays are available, the method returns false.

The `Java_com_example_MyClass_deserialize` method is the entry point for the JNI function. It checks if the class of the object being deserialized is available and creates an `ObjectInputStream` from the byte array. If the deserialization was successful, it returns the deserialized object. If the deserialization failed or the class of the object being deserialized is not available, the method throws a `RuntimeException` with a descriptive error message.

This code uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the queue at a time, and it uses a condition variable to signal when new byte arrays are available for deserialization. It also logs detailed execution times for each deserialization process, which can be useful for performance monitoring and optimization.']

