The function first initializes the metadata array with the total number of chunks based on the size of the input stream. It then iterates over each chunk in the stream, allocating a new buffer for each chunk using `malloc` and copying data from the stream into the buffer. If an error occurs during memory allocation or if the end of the stream is reached before all chunks are initialized, the function breaks out of the loop and returns an error message.

To provide a progress indicator, the function calls a separate `init_progress` function for each chunk that has been initialized. This function calculates the percentage of data chunks that have been initialized so far and prints a progress message to the console.

The function also includes an error handler that handles edge cases such as null pointers and out-of-memory conditions gracefully by printing an error message to the console.

Overall, this code is designed to handle various input streams efficiently, manage memory efficiently, and provide a smooth user experience even for large datasets. It is also modularized and scalable, anticipating future enhancements or modifications to the system.']

