The function then sets the weight of each node to a random value within a specified range using the `rand` function from the C standard library. It also initializes the adjacency list pointers in each node.

Finally, the function defines a `free_memory` function that frees all allocated memory upon function exit. This is important because if the function fails to allocate memory or if it encounters an error while running, it will not be able to free the allocated memory, which can cause memory leaks and other issues.

Note that this code assumes that `num_nodes` and `num_edges` are valid inputs and that the memory allocation functions (`malloc`, `realloc`) return a non-null pointer upon success. If either of these assumptions is not met, the function may fail to allocate memory or may produce incorrect results."]

